schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: Key Terms A-Z
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: concept
  summary: >-
    An A-Z glossary of beginner web3 and Regen terms (Airdrop through Yield
    Farming) — wallets and keys, DeFi mechanics (APR, liquidity, staking),
    DAOs and governance tokens, and crypto slang (FOMO, rug pull, moon). Ends
    with a security reminder never to share a seed phrase or private key.
  audience: beginners new to web3 and regenerative finance
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      glossary brevity vs. accuracy — each term is a one- or two-sentence
      simplification of concepts (e.g. Proof of Stake, oracles) that have
      much deeper technical and economic nuance elsewhere in the toolkit
  related_concepts:
    - ReFi (Regenerative Finance)
    - Tokenomics
    - DAO
    - Layer 2
  related_resources: []
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/key-terms-a-z.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  notes: >-
    Source is a flat A-Z glossary (~80 terms) authored by Tej Pipeline for
    the "Sarreya programs" cohort; captured whole as one encyclopedia entry
    rather than one object per term.

# assessed-but-excluded:
#   - Each individual glossary term (Airdrop, APR, Bridge, dApp, ENS, Gas,
#     KYC, Mempool, Oracle, Snapshot, Whale, etc.) was NOT split into its own
#     concept-lineage object — ~80 short definitions with no distinct source
#     traditions or tensions of their own would create sprawl for no benefit.
#     Two terms with genuine lineage/tension worth preserving (ReFi,
#     Tokenomics) were pulled out separately; see 02- and 03- candidates.
#   - Tool/platform mentions inside definitions (Curve, Discord, MetaMask-
#     adjacent terms) were not lifted to standalone resource objects — they
#     are incidental to the definition, not the glossary's subject.
#   - The closing security reminder ("never share your seed phrase") was not
#     split into a claim-evidence object — it is a plain safety instruction,
#     not a claim needing evidence/uncertainty tracking.
